sify her money and plan tomorrow’s payment?","context":"Use the information in the situation. Choose a response, then examine its consequences.","correct":0,"unit":"","hint":"Distinguish earned money from received support.","consequence":"After K500 accommodation, K50 remains. Review food and transport needs before making another commitment.","options":[{"text":"K150 earned, K400 received; K550 available","feedback":"Both confirmed amounts can be used now. The promised K300 cannot yet cover a bill."},{"text":"K850 available because all three payments count","feedback":"K300 is still expected. Counting it now hides a timing shortfall."},{"text":"Only K150 counts because family support is not earned","feedback":"Received support still provides usable funds once it arrives."}],"feedback":{"correctTitle":"Your decision accounts for the constraint","incorrectTitle":"Consider the consequence","correct":"Both confirmed amounts can be used now. The promised K300 cannot yet cover a bill.","low":"K300 is still expected. Counting it now hides a timing shortfall.","high":"Received support still provides usable funds once it arrives."}},"checkpoint":[{"id":"lesson-1-1-q1","prompt":"Which is irregular earned income?","options":[{"text":"Occasional paid tutoring","feedback":"Payment for work is earned, even when dates and amounts vary."},{"text":"A fixed family gift","feedback":"This is received support, not pay for work."},{"text":"A loan from a friend","feedback":"A loan must be repaid; it is not earnings."}],"correct":0,"explanation":"Payment for work is earned, even when dates and amounts vary."},{"id":"lesson-1-1-q2","prompt":"K240 is in cash, K160 has arrived in a wallet and K200 is promised. What is available now?","options":[{"text":"K600","feedback":"K200 has not arrived."},{"text":"K160","feedback":"Accessible cash also counts."},{"text":"K400","feedback":"K240 + K160 = K400; exclude the promise."}],"correct":2,"explanation":"K240 + K160 = K400; exclude the promise."},{"id":"lesson-1-1-q3","prompt":"Support is delayed and rent is due. What is a useful next step?","options":[{"text":"Assume all irregular income will stop forever","feedback":"A delay is uncertainty to plan for, not proof that no future income will arrive."},{"text":"Check available funds and discuss timing with the landlord","feedback":"Communicating early and checking the shortfall makes the constraint visible."},{"text":"Record the promise as received","feedback":"An entry cannot make delayed money available."}],"correct":1,"explanation":"Communicating early and checking the shortfall makes the constraint visible."}],"summaryTitle":"Bring the ideas together","takeaways":["Distinguish earned money from received support.","Use confirmed funds for immediate commitments.","Plan dates as well as amounts."]}'::jsonb,content='Income is money coming in, such as pay for work or support received. Earned income comes from work or business; received support may be a gift or allowance. Either may be regular or irregular. A promised payment is expected income, not money available today. Write down the source, amount, arrival date and confidence in that date. Borrowed money increases cash but creates a repayment obligation; it is not earnings. For a small trading business, sales receipts are not all profit because stock and other costs must be covered.',revision=1,estimated_minutes=10,is_published=true where id=lid;
