ailable resources and obligations","The timing and consequences","Information that still needs verification"]},"concept":{"title":"Understand the decision","introduction":"An expense is money used for a good, service or obligation. Fixed expenses stay broadly stable over a chosen period; variable expenses change with usage or prices. Frequency is a separate question: a variable cost may recur daily, while a fixed examination fee may occur once. Record the amount, date, category and purpose soon after payment. Categories help you see patterns; they are not judgements about a person.","takeaway":"Separate amount stability from frequency.","definitions":[{"term":"Fixed expense","meaning":"A cost that is broadly stable for the planned period.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."},{"term":"Variable expense","meaning":"A cost that changes with consumption or circumstances.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."},{"term":"Occasional expense","meaning":"A cost that does not occur every regular budget period.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."}]},"example":{"title":"Work through the situation","introduction":"Follow the reasoning. These figures illustrate the decision; they are not current prices, fees or product recommendations.","income":0,"rows":[],"steps":["Five lunches: 5 × K20 = K100.","Four trips: 4 × K15 = K60. Printing: K40.","Weekly purchases total K100 + K60 + K40 = K200. Keep monthly accommodation separate until comparing the same period."],"showBudgetTable":false},"practice":{"kind":"choice","prompt":"Which record accurately describes the week?","context":"Use the information in the situation. Choose a response, then examine its consequences.","correct":0,"unit":"","hint":"Separate amount stability from frequency.","consequence":"Weekly purchases total K100 + K60 + K40 = K200. Keep monthly accommodation separate until comparing the same period.","options":[{"text":"Food K100, transport K60, study materials K40; total K200","feedback":"The record captures every purchase once under useful categories."},{"text":"Food K20, transport K15, printing K40; total K75","feedback":"This counts only one lunch and one trip."},{"text":"Accommodation K300 is the weekly purchase total","feedback":"Accommodation is a separate monthly obligation, not these weekly purchases."}],"feedback":{"correctTitle":"Your decision accounts for the constraint","incorrectTitle":"Consider the consequence","correct":"The record captures every purchase once under useful categories.","low":"This counts only one lunch and one trip.","high":"Accommodation is a separate monthly obligation, not these weekly purchases."}},"checkpoint":[{"id":"lesson-1-3-q1","prompt":"Can an expense be both variable and regular?","options":[{"text":"Only if paid through a bank","feedback":"Payment method does not determine cost frequency."},{"text":"Yes, daily transport can vary by number of trips","feedback":"Frequency and amount stability are different dimensions."},{"text":"No, regular always means fixed","feedback":"A repeated payment may change in amount."}],"correct":1,"explanation":"Frequency and amount stability are different dimensions."},{"id":"lesson-1-3-q2","prompt":"Three K12 snacks and K24 printing total how much?","options":[{"text":"K60","feedback":"3 × K12 + K24 = K60."},{"text":"K36","feedback":"That omits printing."},{"text":"K48","feedback":"That counts only two snacks."}],"correct":0,"explanation":"3 × K12 + K24 = K60."},{"id":"lesson-1-3-q3","prompt":"Why keep dates when recording costs?","options":[{"text":"To make the spending disappear","feedback":"Recording improves visibility but does not undo a payment."},{"text":"To prove all small purchases are bad","feedback":"Small purchases have different purposes; records support informed choices."},{"text":"To compare spending within the correct budget period","feedback":"Dates prevent mixing a week’s costs with a different month."}],"correct":2,"explanation":"Dates prevent mixing a week’s costs with a different month."}],"summaryTitle":"Bring the ideas together","takeaways":["Separate amount stability from frequency.","Add repeated small costs.","Record dates and categories consistently."]}'::jsonb,content='An expense is money used for a good, service or obligation. Fixed expenses stay broadly stable over a chosen period; variable expenses change with usage or prices. Frequency is a separate question: a variable cost may recur daily, while a fixed examination fee may occur once. Record the amount, date, category and purpose soon after payment. Categories help you see patterns; they are not judgements about a person.',revision=1,estimated_minutes=10,is_published=true where id=lid;
