 this decision?","choices":["Available resources and obligations","The timing and consequences","Information that still needs verification"]},"concept":{"title":"Understand the decision","introduction":"A budget period is the span your plan covers. Planned income records what you anticipate; confirmed income records what actually arrived. Allocate essentials with their due dates before optional costs. A monthly plan may need weekly cash-flow checks when support arrives in parts. Unallocated money is the planned income minus allocations, not proof that the same cash is already available. When essentials exceed confirmed funds, identify the gap and discuss timing or support; a budget cannot manufacture income.","takeaway":"Separate expected income from received money.","definitions":[{"term":"Allocation","meaning":"An amount set aside for a category.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."},{"term":"Cash-flow timing","meaning":"When money arrives relative to when costs are due.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."}]},"example":{"title":"Work through the situation","introduction":"Follow the reasoning. These figures illustrate the decision; they are not current prices, fees or product recommendations.","income":0,"rows":[],"steps":["Full allocations: K600 + K400 + K200 + K100 + K100 = K1,400 against K1,500 expected.","With only K900 confirmed, full allocations exceed available money by K500.","A first-period plan could reserve K600 rent, K150 food, K80 transport, K40 data and K30 materials: exactly K900. This is feasible only if reduced amounts cover the period; discuss any remaining essential gap."],"showBudgetTable":false},"practice":{"kind":"allocation","prompt":"Revise Mutinta’s allocations after the delay","context":"Only K900 is available now. Plan for the immediate period: accommodation needs K600, food at least K120, transport at least K60, data at least K30 and study materials at least K20. Allocate all five categories; you may keep some money unallocated. These reduced needs cover only this first period, not the full month.","correct":0,"unit":"K","allocation":{"available":900,"fields":[{"id":"accommodation","label":"Accommodation","minimum":600},{"id":"food","label":"Food","minimum":120},{"id":"transport","label":"Transport","minimum":60},{"id":"data","label":"Data","minimum":30},{"id":"materials","label":"Study materials","minimum":20}]},"hint":"The minimum immediate needs total K830. Decide how to use or reserve the remaining K70. The delayed K600 is not available yet.","consequence":"This is a temporary plan. Compare it with the full K1,400 monthly needs and revisit the remaining period when support arrives. If it does not arrive, discuss unmet essentials and timing; a budget cannot create income.","feedback":{"correctTitle":"Your plan fits the immediate constraints","incorrectTitle":"Review the plan’s gaps","correct":"","low":"","high":""}},"checkpoint":[{"id":"lesson-2-2-q1","prompt":"What is a budget allocation?","options":[{"text":"A payment already made","feedback":"An allocation is a plan; a transaction records an actual payment."},{"text":"A guaranteed future transfer","feedback":"A plan does not guarantee incoming support."},{"text":"Money planned for a category","feedback":"An allocation gives a planned purpose to funds."}],"correct":2,"explanation":"An allocation gives a planned purpose to funds."},{"id":"lesson-2-2-q2","prompt":"Planned income is K1,200 and allocations are K1,050. How much is unallocated?","options":[{"text":"K2,250","feedback":"Adding allocations does not produce a remainder."},{"text":"K150","feedback":"K1,200 − K1,050 = K150 planned remainder."},{"text":"K1,050","feedback":"That is allocated."}],"correct":1,"explanation":"K1,200 − K1,050 = K150 planned remainder."},{"id":"lesson-2-2-q3","prompt":"Which plan handles irregular support best?","options":[{"text":"Use due dates and revise when payments arrive or change","feedback":"Dates connect the monthly plan to immediate commitments."},{"text":"Spend expected income before it arrives","feedback":"This can create a cash shortfall."},{"text":"Avoid budgeting until income becomes regular","feedback":"A flexible plan can be useful precisely because income is irregular."}],"correct":0,"explanation":"Dates connect the monthly plan to immediate commitments."}],"summaryTitle":"Bring the ideas together","takeaways":["Separate expected income from received money.","Allocate essentials by due date.","Revisit the plan when support changes."],"action":{"actionType":"open_budget","destination":"create","label":"Create My Budget","optional":true}}'::jsonb,content='A budget period is the span your plan covers. Planned income records what you anticipate; confirmed income records what actually arrived. Allocate essentials with their due dates before optional costs. A monthly plan may need weekly cash-flow checks when support arrives in parts. Unallocated money is the planned income minus allocations, not proof that the same cash is already available. When essentials exceed confirmed funds, identify the gap and discuss timing or support; a budget cannot manufacture income.',revision=1,estimated_minutes=10,is_published=true where id=lid;
lid:=null; select id into lid from public.lessons where content_key='lesson-2-3';
if lid is null then if (select count(*) from public.lessons where module_id=mid and sort_order=3)>1 then raise exception 'Ambiguous lesson order: map content keys manually'; end if; select id into lid from public.lessons where module_id=mid and sort_order=3; end if;
if lid is null then lid:='5b9cc0bc-9115-4253-abbc-2ae1fa30814c'::uuid; insert into public.lessons(id,module_id,title,content,sort_order) values(lid,mid,'Tracking Expenses','',3); end if;
if exists(select 1 from public.lesson_progress where lesson_id=lid and player_state is not null) and exists(select 1 from public.lessons where id=lid and structured_content is distinct from '{"id":"lesson-2-3","moduleId":"module-2","title":"Tracking Expenses","version":1,"estimatedMinutes":10,"sourceStatus":"reviewed","citations":["nsfe","ilo","worldbank","finscope","toolkit","council"],"references":[{"source":"nsfe","section":"Priority programmes for children and youth; tertiary education","concept":"Practical financial capability for students"},{"source":"ilo","section":"Session 7: Budget and budgeting","concept":"Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting."},{"source":"worldbank","section":"Financial capability and its relationship with inclusion","concept":"Zambian context for tracking expenses"},{"source":"finscope","section":"7.2 Cash-flow Management","concept":"Context for original student scenarios, not current product claims"},{"source":"toolkit","section":"Knowledge, behaviour and attitudes","concept":"Checkpoint design: understanding, application and reasoning"},{"source":"council","section":"Session 9: Control Spending; Session 10: Money In and Money Out","concept":"Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting. Original scenario and exercise; no manual activity reproduced."}],"introduction":"Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting.","outcomes":["Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting."],"situation":{"title":"Tracking Expenses in student life","body":"Tendai has K500 in a wallet. She withdraws K200 into cash, paying an invented K5 fee, then spends K70 cash on food and K40 from the wallet on data. These figures illustrate records, not any provider’s tariff. All amounts and people in this lesson are fictional.","figures":[],"reflection":"What would you check before making this decision?","choices":["Available resources and obligations","The timing and consequences","Information that still needs verification"]},"concept":{"title":"Understand the decision","introduction":"A transaction records a movement of money. An expense pays for something; income adds new funds; a transfer moves your own money between places. A cash withdrawal is not itself a purchase. Record the fee separately as an expense and record purchases when they happen. Otherwise both the withdrawal and the food bought with it may be counted as spending. Reconcile records by comparing the calculated total with actual balances across all places.","takeaway":"Transfers are not expenses.","definitions":[{"term":"Transfer","meaning":"Movement between your own accounts or cash holdings.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."},{"term":"Transaction fee","meaning":"A charge for a service, recorded as an expense.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."},{"term":"Reconciliation","meaning":"Checking records against balances and receipts.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."}]},"example":{"title":"Work through the situation","introduction":"Follow the reasoning. These figures illustrate the decision; they are not current prices, fees or product recommendations.","income":0,"rows":[],"steps":["After withdrawal and fee: wallet K295, cash K200; combined K495.","After purchases: wallet K255, cash K130; combined K385.","Expenses total K5 + K70 + K40 = K115. K500 − K115 = K385; the K200 transfer is not another expense."],"showBudgetTable":false},"practice":{"kind":"choice","prompt":"What should Tendai record as total expenses?","context":"Use the information in the situation. Choose a response, then examine its consequences.","correct":0,"unit":"","hint":"Transfers are not expenses.","consequence":"Expenses total K5 + K70 + K40 = K115. K500 − K115 = K385; the K200 transfer is not another expense.","options":[{"text":"K115: fee, food and data","feedback":"The K200 withdrawal changed location; K115 left her combined funds."},{"text":"K315 including the withdrawal","feedback":"That counts transferred money and its later use twice."},{"text":"K110 excluding the fee","feedback":"The K5 fee also reduced available money."}],"feedback":{"correctTitle":"Your decision accounts for the constraint","incorrectTitle":"Consider the consequence","correct":"The K200 withdrawal changed location; K115 left her combined funds.","low":"That counts transferred money and its later use twice.","high":"The K5 fee also reduced available money."}},"checkpoint":[{"id":"lesson-2-3-q1","prompt":"Which is a transfer?","options":[{"text":"Receiving K100 as a gift","feedback":"That adds new funds rather than moving existing funds."},{"text":"Moving K100 from your wallet into your own cash","feedback":"Ownership stays with you; only location changes."},{"text":"Paying K100 for a textbook","feedback":"That is an expense."}],"correct":1,"explanation":"Ownership stays with you; only location changes."},{"id":"lesson-2-3-q2","prompt":"Start with K300, transfer K80 to cash, pay K4 fee and K50 food. What remains in total?","options":[{"text":"K246","feedback":"K300 − K4 − K50 = K246."},{"text":"K166","feedback":"That incorrectly subtracts the transfer."},{"text":"K250","feedback":"That omits the fee."}],"correct":0,"explanation":"K300 − K4 − K50 = K246."},{"id":"lesson-2-3-q3","prompt":"A record and a wallet balance differ. What should you do?","options":[{"text":"Invent an income entry to force agreement","feedback":"An invented transaction conceals the cause."},{"text":"Delete all cash purchases","feedback":"Valid cash purchases remain expenses."},{"text":"Compare dates, fees and receipts before correcting the record","feedback":"Reconciliation identifies omissions or duplication."}],"correct":2,"explanation":"Reconciliation identifies omissions or duplication."}],"summaryTitle":"Bring the ideas together","takeaways":["Transfers are not expenses.","Record service fees once.","Check combined balances against records."],"action":{"actionType":"open_budget","destination":"transaction","label":"Record a Transaction","optional":true}}'::jsonb) then raise exception 'Lesson content changed after progress: author a revision migration'; end if;
update public.lessons set content_key='lesson-2-3',title='Tracking Expenses',summary='Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting.',structured_content='{"id":"lesson-2-3","moduleId":"module-2","title":"Tracking Expenses","version":1,"estimatedMinutes":10,"sourceStatus":"reviewed","citations":["nsfe","ilo","worldbank","finscope","toolkit","council"],"references":[{"source":"nsfe","section":"Priority programmes for children and youth; tertiary education","concept":"Practical financial capability for students"},{"source":"ilo","section":"Session 7: Budget and budgeting","concept":"Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting."},{"source":"worldbank","section":"Financial capability and its relationship with inclusion","concept":"Zambian context for tracking expenses"},{"source":"finscope","section":"7.2 Cash-flow Management","concept":"Context for original student scenarios, not current product claims"},{"source":"toolkit","section":"Knowledge, behaviour and attitudes","concept":"Checkpoint design: understanding, application and reasoning"},{"source":"council","section":"Session 9: Control Spending; Session 10: Money In and Money Out","concept":"Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting. Original scenario and exercise; no manual activity reproduced."}],"introduction":"Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting.","outcomes":["Separate transfers, fees and purchases without double-counting."],"situation":{"title":"Tracking Expenses in student life","body":"Tendai has K500 in a wallet. She withdraws K200 into cash, paying an invented K5 fee, then spends K70 cash on food and K40 from the wallet on data. These figures illustrate records, not any provider’s tariff. All amounts and people in this lesson are fictional.","figures":[],"reflection":"What would you check before making this decision?","choices":["Available resources and obligations","The timing and consequences","Information that still needs verification"]},"concept":{"title":"Understand the decision","introduction":"A transaction records a movement of money. An expense pays for something; income adds new funds; a transfer moves your own money between places. A cash withdrawal is not itself a purchase. Record the fee separately as an expense and record purchases when they happen. Otherwise both the withdrawal and the food bought with it may be counted as spending. Reconcile records by comparing the calculated total with actual balances across all places.","takeaway":"Transfers are not expenses.","definitions":[{"term":"Transfer","meaning":"Movement between your own accounts or cash holdings.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."},{"term":"Transaction fee","meaning":"A charge for a service, recorded as an expense.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."},{"term":"Reconciliation","meaning":"Checking records against balances and receipts.","detail":"Apply this distinction to the situation and worked example before choosing your response."}]},"example":{"title":"Work through the situation","introduction":"Follow the reasoning. These figures illustrate the decision; they are not current prices, fees or product recommendations.","income":0,"rows":[],"steps":["After withdrawal and fee: wallet K295, cash K200; combined K495.","After purchases: wallet K255, cash K130; combined K385.","Expenses total K5 + K70 + K40 = K115. K500 − K115 = K385; the K200 transfer is not another expense."],"showBudgetTable":false},"practice":{"kind":"choice","prompt":"What should Tendai record as total expenses?","context":"Use the information in the situation. Choose a response, then examine its consequences.","correct":0,"unit":"","hint":"Transfers are not expenses.","consequence":"Expenses total K5 + K70 + K40 = K115. K500 − K115 = K385; the K200 transfer is not another expense.","options":[{"text":"K115: fee, food and data","feedback":"The K200 withdrawal changed location; K115 left her combined funds."},{"text":"K315 including the withdrawal","feedback":"That counts transferred money and its later use twice."},{"text":"K110 excluding the fee","feedback":"The K5 fee also reduced available money."}],"feedback":{"correctTitle":"Your decision accounts for the constraint","incorrectTitle":"Consider the consequence","correct":"The K200 withdrawal changed location; K115 left her combined funds.","low":"That counts transferred money and its later use twice.","high":"The K5 fee also reduced available money."}},"checkpoint":[{"id":"lesson-2-3-q1","prompt":"Which is a transfer?","options":[{"text":"Receiving K100 as a gift","feedback":"That adds new funds rather than moving existing funds."},{"text":"Moving K100 from your wallet into your own cash","feedback":"Ownership stays with you; only location changes."},{"text":"Paying K100 for a textbook","feedback":"That is an expense."}],"correct":1,"explanation":"Ownership stays with you; only location changes."},{"id":"lesson-2-3-q2","prompt":"Start with K300, transfer K80 to cash, pay K4 fee and K50 food. What remains in total?","options":[{"text":"K246","feedback":"K300 − K4 − K50 = K246."},{"text":"K166","feedback":"That incorrectly subtracts the transfer."},{"text":"K250","feedback":"That omits the fee."}],"correct":0,"explanation":"K300 − K4 − K50 = K246."},{"id":"lesson-2-3-q3","prompt":"A record and a wallet balance differ. What should you do?","options":[{"text":"Invent an income entry to force agreement","feedback":"An invented transaction conceals the cause."},{"text":"Delete all cash purchases","feedback":"Valid cash purchases remain expenses."},{"text":"Compare dates, fees and receipts before correcting the record","feedback":"Reconciliation identifies omissions or duplication."}],"correct":2,"explanation":"Reconciliation identifies omissions or duplication."}],"summaryTitle":"Bring the ideas together","takeaways":["Transfers are not expenses.","Record service fees once.","Check combined balances against records."],"action":{"actionType":"open_budget","destination":"transaction","label":"Record a Transaction","optional":true}}'::jsonb,content='A transaction records a movement of money. An expense pays for something; income adds new funds; a transfer moves your own money between places. A cash withdrawal is not itself a purchase. Record the fee separately as an expense and record purchases when they happen. Otherwise both the withdrawal and the food bought with it may be counted as spending. Reconcile records by comparing the calculated total with actual balances across all places.',revision=1,estimated_minutes=10,is_published=true where id=lid;
